        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          1. The Roman & Stoic Origins of Memento Mori and Amor Fati
        </h2>
        <p>
          During the Roman Republic and Empire, when a victorious general returned from military conquest to celebrate a triumphal parade through the streets of Rome, a state slave was positioned directly behind him in the golden chariot. As the crowds cheered and showered the conqueror with laurel wreaths, the slave’s sole duty was to repeatedly whisper into his ear: <em>“Respice post te. Hominem te esse memento. Memento mori.”</em> (“Look behind you. Remember you are a man. Remember you must die.”)
        </p>
        <p>
          In classical Stoicism—developed by Zeno of Citium and immortalized by the Roman triumvirate of <strong>Seneca the Younger, Epictetus, and Emperor Marcus Aurelius</strong>—death was not feared as an evil. Instead, mortality was revered as nature’s ultimate equalizing force and the profound catalyst for intentional, virtuous living. To meditate upon death is to instantly dismantle ego, greed, and procrastination.
        </p>
        <p>
          Coupled with <em>Memento Mori</em> is the sister doctrine of <strong>Amor Fati</strong> (“Love of Fate”). The Stoic does not merely tolerate the finite number of weeks allotted to them; they embrace every joy, hardship, triumph, and tragedy as the exact raw material necessary to forge an unshakeable character.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Seneca's De Brevitate Vitae -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          2. Seneca’s De Brevitate Vitae: Why Life is Long If You Know How to Live It
        </h2>
        <p>
          In his philosophical treatise <em>De Brevitate Vitae</em> (“On the Shortness of Life”), written around 49 AD to his friend Paulinus, Seneca addresses the universal human complaint that nature has granted us too fleeting an existence:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 font-serif italic text-sm my-4 border border-zinc-800">
          “It is not that we have a short time to live, but that we waste a lot of it. Life is long enough, and a sufficiently generous estimate has been given to us for the highest achievements if it were all well invested.”
        </div>
        <p>
          Seneca observed that human beings are fiercely protective of their physical money and real estate, yet astonishingly reckless with the only asset they can never recover: <strong>time</strong>. We squander decades on trivial social obligations, endless corporate vanity, toxic disputes, and idle pastimes, arriving at the end of life with only the illusion of longevity:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>The Busy Idler:</strong> Seneca criticized those who are constantly consumed with bustling activity—attending banquets, managing investments, and courting favors—without ever pausing to engage in philosophical contemplation or deep living.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The Procrastination Trap:</strong> Putting off authentic living until retirement age (e.g. “At 60 I will retire and write”) assumes a guarantee of future time that no mortal possesses.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Living in the Present:</strong> The present moment is the only slice of time over which human agency exerts control. The past is unalterable; the future is uncertain.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Time Perception Psychology -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          3. The Psychology of Time Perception: Why Years Accelerate with Age
        </h2>
        <p>
          Almost every adult observes that time appears to accelerate exponentially as they grow older. Summer vacations that felt like eternal epochs during childhood seem to vanish in the blink of an eye during middle age. Cognitive neuroscience and perceptual psychology explain this through two primary mechanisms:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>Janet's Proportional Theory:</strong> Formulated by philosopher Paul Janet, subjective time is evaluated relative to the total duration of life already experienced. For a 5-year-old child, one single year represents 20% of their entire cognitive existence. For a 50-year-old adult, that same calendar year represents merely 2% of lived experience—making it feel subjectively ten times faster.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Neuro-Cognitive Novelty & Episodic Memory Density:</strong> The human hippocampus and amygdala encode rich, detailed episodic memory traces when confronted with novel environments, first-time experiences, and sensory surprises. In childhood, nearly everything is new. In adulthood, daily life settles into predictable neurological automation (the daily commute, routine office tasks), causing the brain to aggregate weeks of memory into compressed, featureless memory chunks.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>The Stoic Remedy:</strong> To slow down the subjective passage of time, introduce deliberate cognitive novelty: learn new skills, travel to unfamiliar landscapes, engage in creative production, and practice mindful presence.
        </p>
      </section>

